Build with the FontForge we package instead of the FontForge of 2019 that
upstream compiles from source in CI (FONTFORGE_COMMIT aa062f4 of 2019-11-22,
plus fontforge/fontforge#4232). FontForge rewrote its stroker in 20200314,
and four of its behaviours changed under the generator:

 1. layer.stroke() can leave a degenerate open contour
    (fontforge/fontforge#4490, reported by the Duployan author in 2020, open).
 2. Overlap removal inside glyph.stroke() crashes in FindMonoContaining on
    some inputs (fontforge/fontforge#5357, same author, 2024, open; the
    crashing input on x86_64 is the one in that report). Which inputs crash
    differs by architecture: aarch64 crashes on a circle with an exit line
    that x86_64 strokes fine, with the same coordinates, and no rounding
    avoided it there (1/1000, 1/100 and 1/10 all crashed on a Raspberry Pi 5
    and in CI). So every stroke and overlap-removal call runs in a forked
    child that sends the resulting points back over a pipe; if the child
    dies, the parent tries again on copies rounded to 1/100, 1/10 and 1 unit
    and, for a stroke, finally on a copy nudged by a thousandth of a unit
    (the aarch64 case survives at 1/100). If every attempt at a stroke dies
    the build fails, naming the input; if every attempt at an overlap
    removal dies, the overlapping contours are kept and a warning printed.
    A Python exception inside the child is passed back and re-raised in the
    parent, so a generator bug still stops the build as it always did.
    In-process the same crash would take the build with it. Cost: the build
    takes about six minutes per weight instead of one. The child's
    faulthandler trace ("Fatal Python error: Segmentation fault") appears in
    the build log when this happens; it is the child.
    On x86_64 the output is the same as without the guard (same glyphs,
    advances and bounding boxes; two glyphs lose a zero-area two-point
    contour that the release does not have either).
 3. A zero-length path strokes to nothing ("Warning: No stroke output for
    contour") where the old stroker produced the round cap, a filled circle.
    The generator draws every dot that way (Dot.draw), so period, colon,
    U+1BC00 and the other dots came out empty: replace a zero-length contour
    with an explicit circle of the stroke's diameter.
 4. glyph.removeOverlap() on the stenographic period (four round-capped
    strokes through one point) returns a single open contour, which
    _remove_bad_contours then deletes; rounding to 1/100 unit first gives the
    closed contour the 2019 build produced. One- and two-point open leftovers
    are dropped as before.

Also, 'no-hints' in the generate flags makes FontForge 20251009 write a font
without its CFF table (fontforge/fontforge#5678, fixed upstream 2026-02-06),
and the Noto name-table path imported font-v (PyPI, needs GitPython) only to
write "Version 3.002" into name ID 5 for a release build.

The same retry ladder handles a stroke or overlap removal that survives but
comes back with an open contour; set DUPLOYAN_STROKE_LOG=<file> to get a
JSON line per workaround applied (for 3.002 on x86_64: 77 dots per weight,
17/50 stroke retries, 5/1 overlap retries, 149 degenerate leftovers, 0
crashes; on aarch64 one crash per weight at most, retried at 1/100).diff --git a/sources/build.py b/sources/build.py
